{"id":"49830bae-5888-4c6f-bdd2-1b8dbb95d754","object":"chat.completion","created":1784995045,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_dict = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        parent = n['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(n['id'])\\n    root = [n for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None][0]\\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    candidates = [n for n in nodes if n['removable'] and n['id'] != root['id'] and n['symbol'] not in required_symbols]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in candidates:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children_map.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 1:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_list = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removal_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_list\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that returns {\"removed_node_ids\": [...], \"retained_node_ids\": [...]} based on the payload. The task: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" with specifics: deeper nodes before shallower, not removing root, required symbols, or nodes with retained children. Tentatively remove node only if all required symbols remain. Use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nThe payload has nodes with fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. Nodes form a tree (parent references). The algorithm: we want to remove removable nodes that are not required and not root, in order of depth (deeper first) then by id tiebreak (maybe alphabetical? \"node ID as stable tie break\"). After removal, ensure all required symbols still exist in retained nodes. Also cannot remove a node that has retained children (i.e., if a node is removed, its children become orphans? Actually, in parse tree reduction, removing a node typically removes its subtree. But the description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That implies if a node has children that are retained, you cannot remove it because that would remove the children. So we can only remove leaf nodes (or nodes whose entire subtree is being removed). The algorithm: process nodes sorted by depth descending (deepest first), then by id (maybe string compare). For each node, if removable and not required symbol and not root and all required symbols still present after hypothetical removal (i.e., if we remove this node and its subtree, do we still have all required symbols?), then we can remove it. But need to be careful: removing a node also removes its descendants. So we should consider removal of a node only if all its descendants are also removed (either already removed or will be removed as part of this node). The typical approach: iterate nodes in reverse topological order (deepest first) and mark nodes to remove, but when we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree, so any children that are scheduled for removal separately become moot. The description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests we check after removal whether all required symbols still appear in the tree (excluding that node's subtree). Also \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is retained (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove it. So we need to compute which nodes are retained. This is a typical reduction: we decide which nodes to remove, but removal is irreversible. The output should have removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of IDs of nodes that remain after all removals). The order of removal: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (process order? The algorithm processes deeper first, so removal order will be that order). But need to ensure ties are broken by node ID.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build a dictionary from id to node.\n- Build children mapping (parent to children).\n- Identify root (parent null).\n- Mark all nodes initially as \"retained\" = True.\n- Create list of candidate nodes: all nodes except root, and where removable is True, and where symbol not in required_symbols. But also we need to check that node's parent is retained? No, the algorithm checks each node in depth order.\n- Sort candidates: primary key depth descending, secondary key id ascending (string compare). \n- For each candidate in that order:\n  - Check if the node is still retained (might have been removed earlier if parent removed).\n  - Check if node has any retained children (i.e., any child that is retained, and recursively? The spec says \"retained children\" meaning children that are still in the tree. Since we process deeper first, children (deeper) are processed before parent, so if a child was removed, it won't be retained. But if a child was retained because it was not removable, then parent cannot be removed. So check: if any child (direct) that is currently retained, then skip.\n  - Check if removing the subtree rooted at this node would cause loss of required symbols. Compute the set of symbols in the subtree (including the node). Then if after removal, the remaining symbols (from all retained nodes except this subtree) still include all required_symbols, then we can remove. Note: we are removing the whole subtree, even if some descendants were originally not removable? But the spec says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" If a descendant is not removable, it cannot be retained if its parent is removed? Actually, removing a node removes its entire subtree regardless of removable flags. The spec says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That implies we only remove nodes that have no retained children. But a node with a non-removable child would have a retained child, so cannot be removed. So the only nodes we can remove are those with no retained children (i.e., all children are also being removed or have no children). Since we process deeper first, if a non-removable child exists, the parent will have a retained child and be skipped. So the subtree removal only includes nodes that are all removable? Actually, we might remove a node that has removable children; those children are already marked for removal, but if we remove the parent, the children removal is moot. But the children may have been already removed in the order. The algorithm should handle by marking nodes as removed when we decide to remove them and also removing their subtrees. We can implement by setting retained=False for the node and all its descendants. Then when processing later nodes, we skip if not retained.\n\n- For each node we decide to remove, add its id to removed_node_ids list.\n- After processing all candidates, collect all node ids where retained is True, sort them alphabetically (string sort), put in retained_node_ids.\n- Return dict.\n\nWe must ensure we don't remove root (parent null). The root is not in candidates because its removable is false, but also root's id is \"root\"? The root's id is \"root\" with symbol \"root\". required_symbols includes \"root\". So root will be retained.\n\nImplementation details: use standard library only.\n\nLet's write the solve function.\n\nEdge cases: what if a required symbol appears in multiple nodes? required_symbols list includes symbols that must be present in at least one retained node. So we need to check that at least one node with each required symbol remains.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Parse case: case is a dict with keys 'nodes', 'required_symbols'. (and possibly '_case_nonce' but ignore).\n- Build node_dict: {node['id']: node}\n- Build children: default dict list from parent id.\n- Identify root: node with parent None.\n- Initialize retained = {id: True for all nodes}\n- removal_order = []\n- candidate_nodes = [node for node in nodes if node['removable'] and node['id'] != root['id'] and symbol not in required_symbols? Actually, required_symbols is a list of symbols; a node whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed even if removable. So filter out nodes where node['symbol'] in required_symbols.\n- Sort candidate_nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n- Precompute for each node the set of symbols in its subtree? We can compute on the fly by traversing subtree. Since tree size is small, it's fine.\n- Function get_subtree_symbols(node_id, retained_dict): traverse subtree only including nodes that are still retained (though if we are removing, we will mark them as not retained, but for check we consider if we remove this node, all descendants are also removed regardless of their retained status. Actually, if a descendant is not removable but is retained, then parent cannot be removed because it has retained children. So we never get to that point. So when checking removal of node N, all descendants are either already removed (because they were processed earlier and removed) or are still retained (but if they are retained, that means they are not removable or were skipped). If they are still retained and we remove N, they will be removed too,