n subtree of N are just the symbol of N plus any symbols from non-retained children (which are already gone). But those non-retained children were removed earlier and their symbols are not present anywhere. So the only symbol added from the subtree is N's own symbol (since its descendants are already removed). So the check reduces to: if we remove N, does its symbol appear elsewhere? We need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one retained node with that symbol after removal. So we can precompute counts of each symbol among all retained nodes. Initially, count symbols. Then for each candidate N, we want to see if there is another node with N's symbol (if N's symbol is required) because if N is the only node with that required symbol, we cannot remove it. If N's symbol is not required, removal is fine. Also, if N's symbol is required, we need to ensure there is at least one other retained node (not in N's subtree) with that symbol. Since we only remove N (and its subtree of non-retained nodes), the only loss is N's symbol. So we can check if count[symbol] > 1 (or if there is another node with that symbol that is not N). That's simpler.\n\nBut careful: what if a required symbol appears only in a node that is removable and is not the current N, but that node might be removed later? The algorithm processes in order, so if a symbol appears in two nodes and both are removable, the deeper one will be considered first. If it is removed, the count reduces. The later node then sees count==1 and will not be removed. That's correct.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Initialize retained_ids set = all node ids.\n- Initialize symbol_counts: dict from symbol to number of retained nodes with that symbol.\n- For each node, add its symbol to counts.\n- removal_order = []\n- Sort candidate nodes: depth descending, id ascending.\n- For each candidate in that order:\n  - if candidate['id'] not in retained_ids: continue (already removed, maybe by parent).\n  # Check if node has