l is required), n6 (symbol s0 not required). Also note n1 is not removable, n5 is not removable. So candidates: n2, n6. Sort by depth descending: depth2: n4? but n4 excluded, so n6 depth2, then n2 depth1. Within depth2, tie break by id: only n6. Then depth1: n2.\n\nNow process n6: check retained children? n6 has no children; ok. Symbol s0 not required, so can remove. Remove n6: retained_ids remove n6, counts s0 from 1 to 0. removal_order = ['n6'].\nNext n2: retained children? n2 has child n4. n4 is still retained (since not removed). So n2 has retained children -> cannot remove.\nResult: retained_ids: all except n6. Sorted: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']? Wait root id is \"root\", so sorted: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root'].\nremoved: ['n6'].\nBut check symbol s1 required: nodes with s1: n1 and n4, both retained. So ok.\n\nNow check against expected? Not provided, but seems plausible.\n\nBut let's consider if n4 could be removed? n4 has symbol s1 which is required, and count of s1 is 2 (n1 and n4). So could we remove n4? After n6 removed, symbol_counts s1=2. n4 depth2, but removable true, symbol s1 required, count=2 >1, so can remove? But spec says \"never remove a required symbol\". Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a required symbol is defined as symbol? Or node whose symbol is in required_symbols? The wording: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It could mean a node that is itself required (like root) or a node that carries a required symbol. The problem says \"required_symbols\": list of symbols that must be present. So nodes with those symbols are critical. So they should not be removed even if removable. So n4 should not be removed. Our filter excluded nodes with symbol in required_symbols. So n4 is not considered as candidate. Good.\n\nNow consider fixture a-001: similar structure but different values. Candidates: n2 (s2), n6 (s0). n4 excluded because symbol s1 required. Process same: remove n6, then n2 has child n4 retained -> cannot remove. So result same pattern.\n\nFixture a-002 same.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nBut we need to consider if there are cases where a node with required symbol appears only once and is removable? For example, if only n4 had s1 and n1 was not present. Then n4 would be required to keep s1, so shouldn't be removed. Our filter excludes it.\n\nAlso, what about nodes that are not removable but have removable children? They cannot be removed, children may be removed if conditions satisfied.\n\nEdge case: root has children that are removable and not required. Could we remove a direct child of root if it has no retained children? Yes, as long as its symbol is not required. For instance, n2 is child of root, has child n4 which is retained (since n4 not removable due to required symbol), so n2 cannot be removed. If n4 was removable and we removed it, then n2 could be removed.\n\nOur algorithm handles that.\n\nLet's implement.\n\nWe need to ensure we treat id as string for sorting.\n\nImplementation code:\n\nimport json\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Build dicts\n    node_dict = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children_map = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        parent = n['parent']\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(n['id'])\n    # Root is node with parent None\n    root = [n for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None][0]\n    # Initial retained set and symbol counts\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Candidate nodes: removable, not root, symbol not in required_symbols\n    candidates = [n for n in nodes if n['removable'] and n['id'] != root['id'] and n['symbol'] not in required_symbols]\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Check for retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children_map.get(nid, []))\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check if removing this node would lose a required symbol\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 1:\n            continue\n        # Remove node\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained_list = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removal_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_list\n    }\n\nWe must return JSON-serializable object. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to wrap in \"implementation\" JSON string.\n\nThe response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source as a JSON string. I'll generate that.\n\nBut careful: The output contract requires exactly \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". Our result matches.\n\nAlso note that the function signature is solve(case) and will be called once per fixture row. So our implementation is complete.\n\nNow produce the final answer as JSON with \"implementation\" key containing the source code string. Need to escape special characters.\n\nI'll write the source code as a string with proper escaping.\n\nLet me write the source code in a block and then encode as JSON string.\n\nSource code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    node_dict = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children_map = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        parent = n['parent']\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(n['id'])\n    root = [n for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None][0]\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    candidates = [n for n in nodes if n['removable'] and n['id'] != root['id'] and n['symbol'] not in required_symbols]\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children_map.get(nid, []))\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 1:\n            continue\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained_list = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removal_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_list\n    }\n\nNow JSON-escape. I'll embed it in a JSON with key \"implementation\".\n\nI'll produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2054,"completion_tokens":5192,"total_tokens":7246,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4798},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":6},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}